Technically you can do it and JOIN collection tables with regular PG tables, documentdb also supports `->>` and `->` operators on BSON types to get a particular field value but you will need to cast the extracted values to a proper type in PG (BSON supports more types than regular JSON).

e.g. 
```SQL
CREATE TABLE example_table (
    id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    data TEXT
);

INSERT INTO example_table (id, data) VALUES (1, 'First value');
INSERT INTO example_table (id, data) VALUES (2, 'Second value');

SELECT documentdb_api.create_collection('documentdb','patient');
SELECT documentdb_api.insert_one('documentdb','collection1', '{ "joinKey": 1, "data": "First"}');
SELECT documentdb_api.insert_one('documentdb','collection1', '{ "joinKey": 2, "data": "Second"}');

SELECT coll.document, tbl.data 
FROM documentdb_api.collection('documentdb','collection1') coll 
JOIN example_table tbl ON (coll.document->>'joinKey')::int = tbl.id;
                                                  document                                                  |     data     
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+--------------
 { "_id" : { "$oid" : "67acfa68e617768fcf010d70" }, "joinKey" : { "$numberInt" : "1" }, "data" : "First" }  | First value
 { "_id" : { "$oid" : "67acfa74e617768fcf010d71" }, "joinKey" : { "$numberInt" : "2" }, "data" : "Second" } | Second value
(2 rows)
```

Note: The JOIN here will not be utilising any BSON indexes created on collection, again technically it is possible for you to create custom indexes with expressions to support the effective index usage of these cross table / type JOINs

